  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="hub"><div class="hub-box"><div class="hub-title">ASC 350 — Intangibles—Goodwill and Other</div><div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · US GAAP · Subtopics: 350-10 Overall, 350-20 Goodwill, 350-30 General Intangibles, 350-40 Internal-Use Software · USD examples</div></div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">An asset with no separable, reliably estimable useful life</div><div class="principle">Goodwill represents future economic benefits from assets not individually identified — it has NO separable, reliably estimable useful life, making systematic AMORTISATION arbitrary. ASC 350 addresses this by testing goodwill for IMPAIRMENT instead, ensuring its carrying amount never exceeds recoverable value, without imposing an artificial amortisation schedule on an asset that genuinely lacks a determinable consumption pattern.</div></div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">Broadly converged with IFRS, with meaningfully different mechanics</div><div class="principle">ASC 350's core philosophy — goodwill not amortised, tested for impairment — is FULLY converged with IAS 36/IFRS 3. But the MECHANICS of how that impairment test is actually performed, and how finite-lived intangibles and other long-lived assets are tested, differ in genuinely consequential ways (Sections 4, 9, and 16).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Goodwill and intangibles other than goodwill</div><div class="principle">ASC 350 applies to GOODWILL (350-20) recognised in a business combination, GENERAL INTANGIBLE assets other than goodwill (350-30) — both finite-lived and indefinite-lived — and INTERNAL-USE SOFTWARE (350-40), a specific and heavily used category of self-developed intangible asset.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Goodwill — Not Amortised, Tested for Impairment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The converged core principle</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Goodwill is NOT amortised. Instead, it is tested for IMPAIRMENT at least ANNUALLY, and more frequently if events or circumstances indicate it is more likely than not that an impairment has occurred — the SAME fundamental approach IAS 36 requires for goodwill following IFRS 3's business combination accounting.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Reporting Units — the US GAAP Unit of Account</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely different unit of account than IAS 36's CGU</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Goodwill is tested at the REPORTING UNIT level — typically an OPERATING SEGMENT, or ONE LEVEL BELOW an operating segment (a "component"), for which discrete financial information is available and management regularly reviews results. IAS 36, by contrast, tests goodwill at the CASH-GENERATING UNIT (CGU) level — the SMALLEST identifiable group of assets generating cash inflows largely independent of other assets. A reporting unit is typically LARGER and more AGGREGATED than a CGU, meaning the SAME underlying goodwill can be tested at a genuinely different level of granularity under the two frameworks, potentially producing different impairment outcomes for economically identical circumstances.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The One-Step Goodwill Impairment Test (Post-ASU 2017-04)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Simplified in 2017, moving structurally closer to IAS 36</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Before ASU 2017-04, goodwill impairment used a TWO-STEP test: Step 1 compared reporting unit fair value to carrying amount to identify a POTENTIAL impairment; Step 2 required a hypothetical PURCHASE PRICE ALLOCATION to measure the actual impairment amount. ASU 2017-04 ELIMINATED Step 2 — the impairment loss is now simply the excess of the reporting unit's CARRYING AMOUNT over its FAIR VALUE, capped at the total goodwill balance. This is structurally closer to IAS 36's single-step approach, though the BENCHMARK differs: ASC 350 compares against reporting unit FAIR VALUE directly, while IAS 36 compares against RECOVERABLE AMOUNT (the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use) — a subtly different benchmark even after the 2017 simplification.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Qualitative "Step 0" Assessment Option</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A US-specific practical relief with no direct IAS 36 counterpart</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An entity may FIRST perform a QUALITATIVE assessment ("Step 0") of relevant events and circumstances to determine whether it is MORE LIKELY THAN NOT that a reporting unit's fair value is LESS than its carrying amount. If the qualitative assessment concludes it is NOT more likely than not, the entity may BYPASS the quantitative impairment test entirely for that period. IAS 36 has <strong>NO equivalent qualitative bypass option</strong> — it requires QUANTITATIVE annual testing of goodwill (and indefinite-life intangibles) regardless of qualitative indicators, making this a genuine, practically significant reduction in testing burden available only under US GAAP.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>No Reversal of Goodwill Impairment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Fully converged with IAS 36</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Once a goodwill impairment loss is recognised, it is NEVER reversed, even if the reporting unit's fair value subsequently recovers — the SAME absolute prohibition IAS 36 imposes, and the same principle extended to interim periods that IFRIC 10 addresses on the IFRS side (see the IFRIC 10 poster).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Indefinite-Lived Intangibles Other Than Goodwill</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A similarly structured, simplified test</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Indefinite-lived intangible assets other than goodwill (e.g. certain trademarks and licences with no foreseeable limit on their useful life) are similarly NOT amortised, and are tested for impairment at least ANNUALLY using a ONE-STEP quantitative comparison of FAIR VALUE to carrying amount, WITH an equivalent qualitative assessment option available — closely paralleling the goodwill test structure, and broadly converged with how IAS 36 treats indefinite-life intangibles other than goodwill.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Finite-Lived Intangibles — Amortisation and the Two-Step Recoverability Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely significant structural divergence from IAS 36</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Finite-lived intangibles (and other long-lived assets, such as PP&amp;E) are AMORTISED over their useful lives, and tested for impairment ONLY WHEN INDICATORS exist — using US GAAP's distinctive TWO-STEP model: <strong>Step 1 (recoverability test)</strong> compares the asset's carrying amount to the sum of its expected future UNDISCOUNTED cash flows; if the carrying amount is LESS than undiscounted cash flows, the asset PASSES and NO impairment is recognised, regardless of fair value. Only if the asset FAILS this recoverability test does <strong>Step 2</strong> apply — measuring the impairment loss as the excess of carrying amount over FAIR VALUE. IAS 36, by contrast, uses a SINGLE-STEP test — comparing carrying amount DIRECTLY to recoverable amount (a discounted, present-value-based measure) — with no undiscounted-cash-flow "pass" screen. This means an asset that IS economically impaired on a discounted-cash-flow basis can still PASS the US recoverability screen if undiscounted cash flows exceed carrying amount, potentially DELAYING impairment recognition relative to IFRS for the identical underlying asset.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Internal-Use Software (350-40)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A three-stage model, echoing SIC-32's philosophy under IFRS</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Costs during the PRELIMINARY PROJECT stage (conceptual formulation, evaluating alternatives) are EXPENSED. Costs during the APPLICATION DEVELOPMENT stage (once management has authorised funding and it is probable the project will be completed and used as intended) are CAPITALISED. Costs in the POST-IMPLEMENTATION/OPERATION stage (training, routine maintenance) are generally EXPENSED. This stage-based logic is philosophically very similar to the model underlying SIC-32's website cost treatment under IFRS (discussed earlier in this library), even though the two are codified in completely separate, independently drafted pronouncements.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example — Qualitative Assessment Bypassing the Quantitative Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A favourable qualitative outcome</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A reporting unit with a large fair value cushion</div><div class="wk-body">A reporting unit's fair value substantially exceeded its carrying amount at the last quantitative test two years ago. Since then, the reporting unit's operating performance has remained stable or improved, and no significant adverse events have occurred.</div><div class="wk-answer">Management performs a QUALITATIVE assessment considering macroeconomic conditions, industry trends, cost factors, and the reporting unit's own performance, concluding it is NOT more likely than not that fair value is below carrying amount. The entity BYPASSES the quantitative test entirely for the current period — a relief with no equivalent under IAS 36, which would still require a full quantitative recoverable-amount calculation regardless of this favourable qualitative picture.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Worked Example — One-Step Quantitative Goodwill Impairment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Applying the post-2017 simplified test</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A reporting unit with declining fair value</div><div class="wk-body">A reporting unit has a carrying amount of $50,000,000 (including $12,000,000 of goodwill) and an estimated fair value of $44,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Impairment = $50,000,000 − $44,000,000 = $6,000,000, capped at the $12,000,000 goodwill balance — since $6,000,000 is LESS than the goodwill balance, the FULL $6,000,000 is recognised as a goodwill impairment loss. No hypothetical purchase price allocation ("old Step 2") is required — the calculation stops here under the post-2017 simplified model.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Worked Example — the Two-Step Long-Lived Asset Recoverability Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">A finite-lived intangible passing the undiscounted screen</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A customer relationship intangible with declining fair value</div><div class="wk-body">A finite-lived customer relationship intangible has a carrying amount of $8,000,000. Expected future UNDISCOUNTED cash flows total $8,600,000. However, the asset's FAIR VALUE (a discounted, present-value basis) is estimated at only $6,500,000.</div><div class="wk-answer"><strong>Step 1 (recoverability):</strong> Undiscounted cash flows ($8,600,000) EXCEED carrying amount ($8,000,000) — the asset PASSES the recoverability test, and <strong>NO impairment is recognised</strong> under US GAAP, despite the $6,500,000 fair value being well below the $8,000,000 carrying amount.<br><strong>Under IAS 36</strong>, by contrast, recoverable amount ($6,500,000, if that represents the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use) would be compared DIRECTLY to carrying amount ($8,000,000) — producing a <strong>$1,500,000 impairment loss</strong> that US GAAP's undiscounted screen entirely avoids recognising.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 350 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Changes in the carrying amount of goodwill by reporting unit, including impairment losses recognised</span><span>Nature, useful life (or indefinite status), and carrying amount of intangible assets by major class</span><span>Estimated aggregate future amortisation expense for the next five years</span><span>Method and significant assumptions used in impairment testing, and results of any qualitative assessment</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Identifying reporting units and their assigned goodwill</span><span>Whether a qualitative assessment supports bypassing the quantitative test</span><span>Whether an intangible asset genuinely has an indefinite useful life</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of reporting units and indefinite-life intangibles</span><span>Undiscounted and discounted future cash flow projections for finite-lived asset recoverability testing</span><span>Useful lives of finite-lived intangibles and amortisation patterns</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>ASC 350 vs IAS 36/IAS 38 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 350 vs IAS 36 and IAS 38</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 350)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 36/IAS 38)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Goodwill amortisation</span><span>Never amortised, tested for impairment</span><span>Same — fully converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Unit of account for goodwill testing</span><span>Reporting unit (typically operating segment or one level below)</span><span>Cash-generating unit (CGU) — typically SMALLER/more granular</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Goodwill impairment benchmark</span><span>Fair value of the reporting unit</span><span>Recoverable amount (higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use)</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Qualitative bypass option</span><span>AVAILABLE ("Step 0") for goodwill and indefinite-life intangibles</span><span>NOT available — quantitative testing required annually regardless</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Finite-lived/long-lived asset impairment test</span><span>TWO-STEP — undiscounted cash flow recoverability screen, then fair value measurement</span><span>SINGLE-STEP — carrying amount compared directly to recoverable amount</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Reversal of impairment (non-goodwill)</span><span>NOT permitted, once recognised</span><span>PERMITTED (except for goodwill), if recoverable amount subsequently increases</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">The undiscounted-cash-flow recoverability screen for finite-lived and long-lived assets is arguably the single most consequential MECHANICAL difference in this entire comparison — it can genuinely delay impairment recognition under US GAAP relative to IFRS for identical underlying economics, as illustrated in Section 13.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 350 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Goodwill impairment loss</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>One-step test, capped at the goodwill balance, never reversed</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Indefinite-life intangible impairment</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>One-step test, similar to goodwill, but reversible unlike goodwill</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Finite-lived intangible amortisation</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span> <span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Systematic basis over useful life</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Finite-lived intangible impairment (two-step test failed)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Only recognised if the undiscounted recoverability screen is also failed</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Internal-use software capitalisation (application development stage)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Amortised over the software's useful life once in service</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 805</span><span>Business Combinations — supplies the initial recognition and measurement of goodwill this Topic subsequently tests for impairment</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 360</span><span>Property, Plant, and Equipment — uses the SAME two-step recoverability impairment model as finite-lived intangibles under this Topic</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 280</span><span>Segment Reporting — reporting units are typically derived from operating segments identified under this Topic</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary preview</div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Reporting unit</div><div class="glossary-def">The US GAAP unit of account for goodwill impairment testing, typically an operating segment or one level below.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Recoverability test</div><div class="glossary-def">The first step of the two-step US GAAP impairment model for finite-lived intangibles/long-lived assets, comparing carrying amount to undiscounted future cash flows.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Qualitative assessment ("Step 0")</div><div class="glossary-def">An optional preliminary evaluation of qualitative factors that, if favourable, allows an entity to bypass the quantitative goodwill/indefinite-life intangible impairment test.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Application development stage</div><div class="glossary-def">The internal-use software development phase during which costs are capitalised, once funding is authorised and completion/use is probable.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

